 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to authorize the town of Yorktown, county of Westchester, to\ndiscontinue as parklands and lease certain lands",
    "basePrintNo": "S6743",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6743-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Westchester County",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6743\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to authorize the town of Yorktown, county of Westchester, to\ndiscontinue as parklands and lease certain lands\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nAuthorizes the town of Yorktown, county of Westchester, to discontinue\nas parklands and lease at fair market value certain lands.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 authorizes Yorktown to lease land to Homeland Towers, LLC for\nthe purposes of a radio transmission tower facility.\n\nSection 2 requires that Yorktown dedicate the proceeds towards acquiring\nnew parklands or improvement of existing parklands.\n\nSection 3 provides a legal description of the land to be leased.\n\nSection 4 states if the land should be used for purposes other than what\nis described in this act, the lease shall be terminated and the land\nshall be returned to Yorktown and reverted to its previous state\n\nSection 5 states that in the event that Yorktown received federal\nassistance in relation to this parkland, the alienation of the parkland\nwill not occur until the town has met all federal requirements.\n\nSection 6 states the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe alienation of these parklands will allow an approved cell tower to\nbe installed, moving the region closer to achieving its goals set forth\nin its wireless master plan. The cell tower will enhance communication\ncapabilities for residents, businesses and first responders. The town of\nYorktown has requested this bill in compliance with the state's rules\nregarding home rule.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
